WEBVTT

1
00:00:04.400 --> 00:00:08.440
A program can have a mistake in
it. We call that mistake a bug.

2
00:00:08.660 --> 00:00:11.865
Finding the bug and fixing
it is called debugging.

3
00:00:12.365 --> 00:00:15.801
Long ago, a computer was
as big as a whole room.

4
00:00:16.021 --> 00:00:18.854
One day it stopped working,
and nobody knew why.

5
00:00:19.074 --> 00:00:23.114
Inside the machine, the people
found a real insect. A moth!

6
00:00:23.334 --> 00:00:27.375
People already called a mistake
a bug. Now they had a real one!

7
00:00:27.595 --> 00:00:32.471
They stuck the moth in their notebook.
That story made the word famous.

8
00:00:32.971 --> 00:00:36.314
We want the dog to move
right twice, and then jump.

9
00:00:36.534 --> 00:00:42.200
Here is the program for it. Does it
work? You cannot tell just by looking.

10
00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:46.507
So you run it, and watch
closely. Press Run to find out.

11
00:00:46.727 --> 00:00:50.628
The dog moves right. Then it
moves left. Then it jumps.

12
00:00:50.848 --> 00:00:54.888
That is not what we wanted. So
this program has a bug in it.

13
00:00:55.388 --> 00:01:01.657
Why did it go wrong? Compare the program
with what we wanted, block by block.

14
00:01:01.877 --> 00:01:06.150
Block one says move right. We
wanted move right. That one is fine.

15
00:01:06.370 --> 00:01:10.364
Block two says move left.
But we wanted move right.

16
00:01:10.584 --> 00:01:14.670
Block three says jump. We wanted
jump. That one is fine too.

17
00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:18.838
So the second block is the bug.
It says left instead of right.

18
00:01:19.338 --> 00:01:22.867
Now fix it. Take the wrong block
out and put the right one in.

19
00:01:23.087 --> 00:01:26.524
Move right, move right,
jump. That is what we wanted.

20
00:01:26.744 --> 00:01:30.459
Now run it again. Always test
again after you fix something.

21
00:01:30.679 --> 00:01:34.162
The dog moves right, right,
and jumps. The bug has gone.

22
00:01:34.382 --> 00:01:38.190
You found the bug and you fixed
it. That is what debugging means.

23
00:01:38.690 --> 00:01:43.241
An everyday algorithm can have a
bug too. Here is washing your hands.

24
00:01:43.461 --> 00:01:47.734
Turn the tap on. Put soap on
your hands. Then eat the soap?

25
00:01:47.954 --> 00:01:51.947
Eating the soap does not belong
here. That step is the bug.

26
00:01:52.167 --> 00:01:56.394
Take it out. Rub your hands together
instead, then rinse and dry them.

27
00:01:56.614 --> 00:02:00.050
Sometimes a good step is
just in the wrong place.

28
00:02:00.270 --> 00:02:03.289
Rinsing before you rub
washes the soap away.

29
00:02:03.789 --> 00:02:07.922
Even expert programmers write
bugs. Everybody does, every day.

30
00:02:08.142 --> 00:02:12.275
A games maker tests her own
game. A door will not open.

31
00:02:12.495 --> 00:02:16.767
An app keeps closing by itself.
The programmers send a fix.

32
00:02:16.987 --> 00:02:21.074
A rocket's programs are tested
again and again before it flies.

33
00:02:21.574 --> 00:02:24.825
A bug is a mistake in the
program, not in the computer.

34
00:02:25.045 --> 00:02:28.853
Run it and watch it. Compare what
happened with what you wanted.

35
00:02:29.073 --> 00:02:33.485
Find the wrong block, fix it, and
run it again. That is debugging.
